(Chained `but if` plus a trailing `otherwise`, using an append base.  The
first matching branch wins; `otherwise` is the catch-all.)

a list called out is [].
a number called n is 15.
append "x" to out,
    but if n modulo 15 is equal to 0 append "fizzbuzz",
    but if n modulo 3 is equal to 0 append "fizz",
    but if n modulo 5 is equal to 0 append "buzz",
    otherwise append "none".
print out.

a list called out2 is [].
a number called m is 7.
append "x" to out2,
    but if m modulo 15 is equal to 0 append "fizzbuzz",
    but if m modulo 3 is equal to 0 append "fizz",
    but if m modulo 5 is equal to 0 append "buzz",
    otherwise append "none".
print out2.
